F. Ethical screening engine
Implement the screening engine in Python.
Each candidate must receive one of three primary statuses:
ELIGIBLE FOR FURTHER COMPARISON:
Available evidence satisfies the configured mandatory checks. This does not mean the fund is automatically a suitable investment.
EXCLUDED:
Reliable evidence establishes a breach of a mandatory policy rule.
NEEDS INVESTIGATION:
Evidence is missing, outdated, contradictory, ambiguous or insufficient to make a reliable decision.
Store a separate evidence-completeness status so users can distinguish full from partial screening.
The engine must:
Load the active charity policy.
Load the available candidate data and holdings.
Match holdings and issuers to policy sectors and activities.
Apply mandatory exclusions.
Apply configured exposure thresholds.
Distinguish direct and indirect exposure when data supports it.
Detect unknown exposure.
Identify outdated or incomplete disclosures.
Record the rule responsible for each finding.
Generate a structured screening result.
Mandatory exclusions must never be offset by a high sustainability score, strong past returns or low fees.
If a confirmed exclusion breach exists, mark the candidate as excluded even if other data is missing.
If no breach is confirmed but essential evidence is missing, use “Needs investigation,” not “Eligible.”
Never infer that an investment is ethical simply because no adverse information was found.
